    <title type="sort">1</title>
    <desc>Start, stop, configure, connect, rename and delete the bricks of a lab.</desc>
  </info>
  <title>Bricks</title>
  <p>The <gui>Bricks</gui> tab has a row for each brick of the project: its icon, grey while it is stopped; its name; its kind and what matters about it, as <sys>Wire · sw1 ↔ sw2</sys>; its state; a button that starts or stops it; and the button of its menu.</p>
  <terms>
    <title>The states</title>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Running</gui>, <gui>Stopped</gui></title>
      <p>Its program runs, or not.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Starting</gui></title>
      <p>Its program runs once the bricks it plugs into run, and a virtual machine has its disks ready. Meanwhile it counts as running: you can't rename it or delete it, nor quit Virtualbricks.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Not connected</gui></title>
      <p>A plug of the brick goes nowhere, as a wire with a free end: it can't start until you connect it.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Not configured</gui></title>
      <p>A setting that it needs is missing, as the host of a tunnel client: it can't start until you set it.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Error</gui></title>
      <p>A switch wrapper whose folder has no switch to plug into, though the folder is there: it has no socket <file>ctl</file>, or the one that a killed switch leaves. The tooltip of the state says what is wrong, and so does the <gui>Logs</gui> window. It runs again once a switch listens there.</p>
    </item>
  </terms>
  <section id="row">
    <title>Above the list</title>
    <p><gui>New Brick</gui> adds a brick; see <link xref="kinds"/>. The search field finds a brick by name or by kind: press <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>F</key></keyseq>, or start typing in the list. <gui>All</gui> and <gui>Running</gui> switch between all the bricks and only those that run, which then show the number of their process. <gui>Start All</gui> starts every brick that can start, and <gui>Stop All</gui> stops them.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="start">
    <title>Starting and stopping</title>
    <p>The button of a row starts the brick, or stops it. Starting a brick starts first the bricks it plugs into. Stopping a virtual machine asks its system to shut down. Stopping a brick that is starting calls its start off: its program doesn't run, and the bricks it plugs into start all the same.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="settings">
    <title>The settings of a brick</title>
    <p>Double-click a row, or select it and press <key>Enter</key>: the settings of the brick show in place of the list, under a line that names it. <gui>OK</gui> applies them, and is greyed while a setting is wrong, with the first problem beside it. <gui>Cancel</gui> and <key>Esc</key> go back to the list without a change. While the brick runs, a bar over its settings says which of them change at once.</p>
    <figure>
      <title>The settings of a switch</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/switch-settings.png">
        <p>The settings of sw1 in place of the list, under a line that names it: Ports, 32, and Hub mode and Fast spanning tree, off; Help, Cancel and OK at the bottom.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <section id="menu">
    <title>The menu of a brick</title>
    <p>The button at the end of the row, the right button of the mouse, the <key>Menu</key> key and the picture of the <gui>Topology</gui> tab open the same menu:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Start</gui>, <gui>Stop</gui></title>
        <p>As the button of the row.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Configure…</gui></title>
        <p>The settings of the brick; <key>Enter</key> in the list.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Rename…</gui>, <gui>Delete…</gui></title>
        <p>Greyed while the brick runs or starts; <key>F2</key> and <key>Delete</key> in the list. Before a brick goes, the dialog says what goes with it: the bricks plugged into it are unplugged, the events no longer start or stop it, and the private copies of a machine go to the trash.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Duplicate</gui></title>
        <p>A copy of the brick, with its links, named after it with the next free number. The network cards of a machine get new MAC addresses.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Connect To</gui></title>
        <p>The bricks that this one can plug into. Dragging the row of a brick onto the row of another does the same: when one of the two can plug into the other, that row is framed, and they connect when you let go.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>When It Starts</gui>, <gui>When It Stops</gui></title>
        <p>An event to run when the brick starts or stops; see <link xref="events"/>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Resume</gui></title>
        <p>For a virtual machine, start it from the state that <gui>Suspend</gui> saved in its first disk. A machine that runs goes back to that state.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Process</gui></title>
        <p>While the brick runs, what to do with its program; see <link xref="bricks#process"/>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="process">
    <title>The process of a brick</title>
    <p>While a brick runs, its menu has <gui>Process</gui>, named after the number of its process, as <gui>Process 4242</gui>. Its items act on the program of this brick alone, never on the bricks it plugs into. A brick that is starting has no process yet, and a switch wrapper has none of its own: its switch is another program's.</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Open Control Monitor</gui></title>
        <p>Opens the console of the program in a terminal, the one of the <gui>Terminal</gui> setting (see <link xref="settings"/>): the monitor of QEMU for a virtual machine, the console of <cmd>vde_switch</cmd>, <cmd>vde_router</cmd> or <cmd>vde-netemu</cmd> for a switch, a router or a netemu. There you type the commands of the program, as <cmd>info network</cmd> in QEMU or <cmd>port/print</cmd> in a switch; <cmd>help</cmd> lists them. Closing the terminal leaves the program running, but <cmd>quit</cmd> in QEMU and <cmd>shutdown</cmd> in a switch end it, and the brick stops. A tap and a capture interface have no console.</p>
        <p>In the windows of another computer (see <link xref="remote"/>), the terminal opens on your desktop. When your desktop lacks the program it needs, the item is greyed and says which, as <gui>Open Control Monitor (needs unixterm of vde2)</gui>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Pause</gui>, <gui>Continue</gui></title>
        <p><gui>Pause</gui> freezes the program where it is, with the signal SIGSTOP, and <gui>Continue</gui> lets it go on, with SIGCONT. A paused switch passes no traffic, and a paused machine does nothing at all. Nothing shows that a brick is paused: its state stays <gui>Running</gui>. <gui>Stop</gui> may wait until it continues; <gui>Kill</gui> doesn't.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Suspend</gui></title>
        <p>For a virtual machine: saves the state of the machine, its memory and its devices, in its first disk, then asks its system to shut down, as <gui>Stop</gui>. <gui>Resume</gui>, in the menu, starts it again from that state. The state goes into the private copy of the disk, which takes any image, or into the image itself, which must then be qcow2: on a raw one the <gui>Logs</gui> window says that suspend isn't supported.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Reset</gui></title>
        <p>For a virtual machine: its reset button. The system of the machine starts over at once, without shutting down, and what it didn't save is lost. The process stays the same.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Restart</gui></title>
        <p>Stops the brick, as <gui>Stop</gui>, and starts it again, in a new process: the settings that wait for a start take effect, and its events run, <gui>When It Stops</gui> then <gui>When It Starts</gui>. A brick that hasn't stopped after two seconds is killed, as <gui>Kill</gui>: a virtual machine has those two seconds to shut its system down.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Terminate</gui></title>
        <p>For a virtual machine whose system doesn't answer <gui>Stop</gui>: QEMU closes the disks and ends, with the signal SIGTERM, and the system of the machine goes off as when the power is cut. The other bricks have no need of it, since their <gui>Stop</gui> already sends SIGTERM. Greyed in the windows of another computer.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Kill</gui></title>
        <p>The program ends at once, with the signal SIGKILL, which it can't refuse, even paused. It can't clean up either: a switch leaves its socket behind, and QEMU doesn't close the disks of a machine. The last resort, when <gui>Stop</gui>, or <gui>Terminate</gui> for a machine, doesn't stop it.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
